Author:     Ivan Kozik <ivan@ludios.org>
AuthorDate: 2026-09-16 01:40:42 +0000
Commit:     Ivan Kozik <ivan@ludios.org>
CommitDate: 2026-09-16 01:40:42 +0000

    lib: verify frame trailer at the offset limit; error on truncated source and seek table size mismatch
    
    Model-output: Claude Fable 5.1
    
    <prompt>
    
    ok, great. could you please fix that checksum-skip bug? see if you can unit test first
    
    I added a CLAUDE.md just now fwiw
    
    AskUserQuestion "How should the decoder report the two new failure cases: the source hitting EOF inside a frame, and a frame ending at a different decompressed position than its seek table entry says?" -> "New error kinds (Recommended)"
    
    AskUserQuestion "Should the fix also check, at every frame end zstd reports, that the decompressed position equals the seek table's frame end? This is what turns a wrong seek table (review finding 2, silent truncation or misaligned seeks) into an error." -> "Check every frame end (Recommended)"
    
    </prompt>
    
    <slop>
    
    **Fixed and committed on branch `verify-frame-end`.** The decoder now verifies every frame it decompresses to the end, and it errors instead of reporting success on a truncated source or on a seek table that disagrees with the frames.
    
    **The bug.** The decoder's loop stopped as soon as the requested number of bytes had been produced. When a frame's 4-byte checksum landed in a later read than its last content byte, zstd never saw the trailer, so a corrupted frame decoded with exit 0. With the CLI's 131075-byte input buffer that happened whenever a frame's payload filled the buffer exactly. With small reads it happened every time.
    
    **The fix** in [decode.rs](lib/src/decode.rs) and [error.rs](lib/src/error.rs), following your two answers:
    
    - The decoder tracks which frame the zstd context is in and whether that frame is finished. When the offset limit sits at a frame end, it keeps feeding input with no output room until zstd confirms the frame end, which is when the checksum gets checked.
    - At every frame end it compares the decompressed position with the seek table entry and fails with the new `FrameSizeMismatch` error if they differ. A stalled drain, where zstd holds more data than the entry allows, fails the same way.
    - A zero-byte read while more input is needed fails with the new `UnexpectedEof` error instead of spinning forever. That covers the review's finding 8 as a side effect.
    - A limit inside a frame still skips that frame's checksum, as documented.
    
    **Tests written first.** All six failed on the old code and pass now: valid data with 1-byte reads consumes every trailer; a flipped byte is caught with 1-byte and 3-byte reads, with the limit at an inner frame end, and at the exact input-buffer boundary; a source cut inside the last frame errors; a table off by one byte in either direction errors.
    
    **Verification.** Workspace tests, no_std tests, doc tests, clippy pedantic, and fmt are clean. The release CLI rejects the 131,066-byte repro with "Restored data doesn't match checksum". The interop matrix from earlier gives identical results, and Meta's reference decoder still agrees with zeekstd on all library probes.
    
    **Codex review** (gpt-6-astra, xhigh): pending when this commit was made; the message is amended with the outcome afterwards.
    
    **Coordination.** Session zeekstd-47 is fixing the EOF spin on branch `eof-spin` and will rebase onto this commit, keeping only extra tests. Session zeekstd-68 is adding a `From<Error> for io::Error` in the same error.rs, so expect a small merge conflict there.
    
    Next step is a PR from `verify-frame-end`. I did not push.
    
    </slop>

